= shui-tools
Manfred Güntner
:toc:
:toclevels: 2

A small collection of terminal user interface tools for POSIX shell scripts.

The project currently contains three standalone programs:

* *shakey* - wait for a single key press
* *shselect* - interactive selector and pager
* *shedit* - terminal text input widget

All tools are written in portable C89 and have no external dependencies beyond a POSIX-compatible operating system and terminal.

== Why?

I had been missing simple terminal UI building blocks since around 2009, but never found the time to create them.

The goal of this project is to provide a small set of reusable components for POSIX shell scripts: selecting files, entering text, displaying simple menus, or controlling terminal applications without having to rely on tput, ncurses, or large frameworks.

I deliberately chose an old-school approach:

* small standalone executables
* portable C89 source code
* no external dependencies
* simple compilation using a standard C compiler
* suitable for scripts and minimal systems

The focus is not on creating a full-screen text user interface framework, but on providing practical building blocks that can easily be combined in shell scripts.

The software has been successfully tested on:

* Debian GNU/Linux x86_64 (WSL2)
* NetBSD x86_64
* NetBSD arm64
* NetBSD i386

Additional platforms will likely work as long as a reasonably POSIX-compatible terminal environment is available.

== Features

* Single-key input without requiring Enter
* Interactive terminal selection menus
* Lightweight text input widget
* Designed for shell scripting
* No ncurses dependency
* Small standalone executables
* Portable C89 code

== Included Programs

=== shakey

Wait for a single key press or a timeout.

Useful for:

* media player controls
* confirmation dialogs
* keyboard shortcuts
* interruptible sleep operations

Example:

[source,sh]
----
key=$(shakey -t 10 -s -p "Press q to quit")

if [ "$key" = "q" ]; then
    exit 0
fi
----

Wait for up to five seconds and show the pressed key:

[source,sh]
----
key=$(shakey -t 5 -s -p "Press any key")

echo "You pressed: $key"
----

Use shakey as an interruptible sleep:

[source,sh]
----
while true
do
    key=$(shakey -t 60 -s)

    [ "$key" = "q" ] && break

    check_for_updates
done
----

=== shselect

Interactive list selector and pager.

Useful for:

* selecting files
* choosing menu entries
* radio station selectors
* shell-based launchers

Example:

[source,sh]
----
choice=$(
    printf "%s\n" \
        "One" \
        "Two" \
        "Three" |
    shselect -s
)

echo "Selected: $choice"
----

Multiselect example:

[source,sh]
----
selected=$(
    find . -type f |
    shselect -m -s -p "Select files"
)

printf '%s\n' "$selected"
----

Directory navigation menu:

[source,sh]
----
file=$(
    ls -1 |
    shselect -s -p "Select a file"
)

less "$file"
----

=== shedit

Simple text input widget for shell scripts.

Useful for:

* chat clients
* notes
* commit messages
* editing generated text

Example:

[source,sh]
----
message=$(shedit -p "Enter message")
----

Preloading text:

[source,sh]
----
message=$(
    shedit -r10 < template.txt
)
----

Edit generated content before saving:

[source,sh]
----
jq '.' data.json |
    shedit -r20 -p "Review JSON" \
    > data-new.json
----

Simple note editor:

[source,sh]
----
note=$(shedit -r15 -p "Write note")

printf '%s\n' "$note" > note.txt
----

== Building

No makefile is required.

Compile the programs individually using a C89 compiler:

[source,sh]
----
cc shakey.c   -o shakey   -std=c89
cc shselect.c -o shselect -std=c89
cc shedit.c   -o shedit   -std=c89
----

== Installation

Copy the binaries somewhere in your PATH:

[source,sh]
----
install -m755 shakey   /usr/local/bin/
install -m755 shselect /usr/local/bin/
install -m755 shedit   /usr/local/bin/
----

Install the manual pages:

[source,sh]
----
install -d /usr/local/share/man/man1

install -m644 man/shakey.1   /usr/local/share/man/man1/
install -m644 man/shselect.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
install -m644 man/shedit.1   /usr/local/share/man/man1/
----

Update the man page database if required by your system:

[source,sh]
----
mandb
----

== Example: Menu System

A simple shell menu using shselect:

[source,sh]
----
choice=$(
    printf "%s\n" \
        "Play Music" \
        "Settings" \
        "Quit" |
    shselect -s -p "Main Menu"
)

case "$choice" in
    "Play Music")
        echo "Starting player"
        ;;
    "Settings")
        echo "Opening settings"
        ;;
    "Quit")
        exit 0
        ;;
esac
----

== Example: Confirmation Dialog

[source,sh]
----
answer=$(shakey -s -p "Delete file? (y/n) ")

case "$answer" in
    y|Y)
        rm file.txt
        ;;
esac
----

== Example: Text-Based Chat Input

[source,sh]
----
while true
do
    msg=$(shedit -r5 -p "Enter message")

    [ -z "$msg" ] && continue

    send_message "$msg"
done
----

== Documentation

Manual pages are included in the `man/` directory.

[source,sh]
----
man ./man/shakey.1
man ./man/shselect.1
man ./man/shedit.1
----

After installation:

[source,sh]
----
man shakey
man shselect
man shedit
----

== Exit Codes

The tools generally follow standard UNIX conventions and return non-zero values on errors.

Some programs provide additional return codes for script control. See the corresponding man page for details.

For example, shselect returns exit code 10 when the user leaves the selector with `x`, making it possible to implement "back" navigation in shell applications.

